missing.why:
The draft gives a process chain and a cost table but no ramp. Missing:
- a plates/month ramp curve;
- CM selection criteria;
- funding for the US$3.7-9 M per 100k/yr CM capex (verify-cost-market);
- long-lead items (vacuum furnaces, skiving machines, progressive dies);
- OFE strip sources;
- silver exposure (US$13-24 per plate at US$60-72/oz) and a hedge;
- per-SKU die economics;
- Taiwan manufacture blocked until TW I606224 is charted;
- the 10k/yr ramp cost of about US$370-430 for the first 18-24 months.

There is no compliance matrix. Missing:
- IEC/UL 62368-1 liquid-filled-component requirements, which OEMs must certify (UL places them in Annex G, clause G.21 in the edition it cites);
- PED 2014/68/EU Art. 4(3) sound-engineering-practice status;
- RoHS and REACH declarations;
- conflict-minerals reporting for Sn (route C) and W (T2 barrier);
- EU PFAS;
- export classification.
Most material is US Section 232 copper tariffs. As reported, from 8 Jun 2026 there is 50 % on the full value of articles made entirely or almost entirely of copper and 25 % on copper derivatives, with heat-exchanger parts proposed for inclusion. On a TW/VN/MX-built plate that is about US$52-136 per plate (customs value about 1.3x factory cost), as large as or larger than the whole PRISM premium (US$67-99).

Several claims exceed the planned equipment:
- TTV-A is limited to 18 kW (E3), but the scorecard publishes Q capacity of 19.4 (PCM nominal), 23.3, 26.4 and 34.1 kW, plus 25-30 kW cases, and the rig is 25 kW;
- TTV-100 at 15 kW runs AlN heaters at 150 W/cm2, 97 % of their 155 W/cm2 rating;
- the 350 W/cm2 KPI has no heater path (2.3x the AlN rating);
- the confocal pressure-scan station is unbudgeted (US$0.2-0.4 M);
- no ISO/IEC 17025 traceability, transient test, orientation test, or replication of CoolIT's 8-TC layout is defined.

The draft has:
- no named lead customer or letter of intent;
- no NDA envelope (MGX/Kyber tray CAD or the NVIDIA design guide; the research flags these as unobtained);
- no interface control drawing (mount pattern, port positions, load frame, keep-outs, mass limit);
- no qualification route or timeline (NVIDIA RVL / ODM qualification takes 12-18 months to a first PO).
Rank-2 entry is routed through a Group-A house, but NVIDIA standardises the design centrally.

Not covered:
- shipping shock and vibration: a 1.74 kg plate, excluding the 0.3-0.6 kg frame, puts about 510-850 N on the package at 30-50 g, against package windows of 0.3-1 kN;
- the 2 kN TTV clamp versus real retention;
- orientation: vertical blades such as Kyber trap air in 9 mm plenums, dead ends and headers, and fin velocity is only 0.15 m/s;
- a degas/vent procedure at commissioning.
Bow under the 2 kN clamp is deferred to FEA.

The draft compares only with CoolIT. 'Elite' is undefined: PRISM's core R'' is 0.108 cm2K/W (convection plus base), 0.167 with caloric and 0.223 with PCM. Peer-reviewed MMC plates reach 0.065-0.09 total and Mikros/Jabil claims 0.02 core (water, different filtration). Missing from the comparison:
- JetCool SmartPlate (Rjf 0.040-0.058 C/W at 600 W, 1.5 LPM/kW; claims -25 % against microchannel);
- Frore LiquidJet (600 W/cm2 hot spot, 1.7 kPa, 0.87 LPM/kW, -6 to -12 C on Rubin);
- Fabric8/Wiwynn ECAM (4 kW double-sided, '48 % better than skived') and UIUC/Fabric8 (-32 % R, -68 % dP);
- ORNL diamond pin-fin (6.9 mK/W including TIM at 1 kW, 9 kPa);
- ToneCooling GB300 (13 mK/W, 12 kPa);
- AVC/Auras MLCP and Jentech MCL;
- Accelsius (-9 C Tj; 54 C equivalent to 45 C);
- ZutaCore (0.3 LPM/kW, 2.8 kW certified);
- Corintis/Microsoft in-die (-65 % Si rise) and TSMC direct-to-silicon (> 2.6 kW at dT < 63 C);
- CoolIT's own OMNI (> 300 W/cm2, 'up to 60 % lower R');
- Boyd, Vertiv/CoolTera, Motivair, nVent, Delta, Cooler Master (no public plate data; say so).

