missing.why:
Several public claims have no verifying experiment or pass criterion:
- Q capacity above 18 kW, the 350 W/cm2 KPI, the 25-30 kW cases, GPU-class lids, the 10-year fouling life and the 4 kW point (E0 does not include a CoolIT 4 kW-class plate);
- the E2 uniformity metric is per line, not per segment;
- the E1 gate cannot see a single unbonded land strip.

Analysts expect micro-channel lids (Jentech) and micro-channel cold plates (AVC/Auras) to be mainstream from 2027, and Feynman (2028) is a candidate for in-package microfluidics. PRISM's MCL path (T3B) has no design numbers. Also missing:
- CoolIT's likely responses: OMNI, multi-slot/zoned plates after 2028, an Ecolab chemistry bundle, publishing in its own OCP format;
- Nidec WO2026100588 (fin-tip-brazed cover) showing that fin-tip ties are being industrialised;
- how long the moat lasts once the full OCP report is published and filings 2-4 become defensive publications.

D1 asks for approval of a claim that is false: the 4 kW point is lost, and the paste average loses at 100 cm2. The draft never puts these decisions to the manager:
- PKG-A versus the drafted stack;
- the US launch date, given the Split-Flow expiry around Sep 2028;
- the manufacturing site, given the tariff and TW I606224;
- whether the tray cartridge is mandatory (which ends 'drop-in');
- whether to fund P-grade/R+ with its filter kit (no cost or dP given);
- the MCL and two-phase hedges.
D3's budget omits US$0.9-2.1 M.

hostile_attacks:
'Not drop-in.' The R-grade needs:
- a new tray filter; without it your 25 cm2 guard is the loop filter and blinds in about 65 days at 1e-4/mL;
- keyed 20-50 kPa orifices in any mixed rack;
- a tighter commissioning cleanliness spec;
- liquid-metal containment for the stretch tiers;
- a new interface control drawing.
The P-grade 'decisive product' additionally needs ≤ 33 um absolute filtration that fleets do not have.
